e symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (disease)\nAssess disease risk (red rot, smut, rust, leaf spot) from humidity, temperature, and the symptoms the farmer describes. Advise isolation/removal and when to consult a plant pathologist.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 49941 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary:\nIron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous soils, and worsens in successive ratoon crops. IISR documents six treatment options for chlorosis management and identifies the broader set of micronutrients (B, Cu, Fe, S, Mg, Mn, Mo, Zn) affecting cane growth, along with critical soil deficiency thresholds.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations:\n- Farmers on calcareous or high-pH soils (common in parts of Karnataka's black-soil tracts) should watch for chlorosis symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information:\n- Iron chlorosis has been observed in nearly all sugarcane-growing states of India, particularly Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, and Bihar; it is most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.\n- **Six documented treatments for lime-induced iron chlorosis:**\n  1. Foliar spray of 2% FeSO₄ solution combined with 0.5% MnSO₄ and 2% urea, applied two to three times.\n  2. Soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg/ha.\n  3. Soil application of press mud (5 t/ha) combined with iron pyrite (2 t/ha).\n  4. Soil application of farm yard manure (25 t/ha) plus foliar application of 1.5% FeSO₄ with 1% urea at weekly intervals, and 1% ZnSO₄ at monthly intervals.\n  5. Soil application of elemental sulphur at 30–40 kg/ha, applied 3 weeks before planting.\n  6. Foliar spray of Fe-EDTA (50 ppm solution).\n\nSugarcane Disease Management — Main Information:\n- **Red Rot** (fungus: *Colletotrichum falcatum*): the most serious sugarcane disease in India. The most effective control is planting resistant varieties, combined with healthy cultural practices and crop rotation.\n- **Smut** (fungus: *Sporisorium scitamineum*): managed through resistant varieties, seed selection and roguing of infected clumps, hot water treatment (52°C for 2 hours), and/or sett dipping in 0.2% Bavistin (carbendazim) for half an hour before planting.\n- **Wilt** (fungus: *Fusarium sacchari*): seed material should be dipped in 0.2% Bavistin before planting; crop rotation with coriander or mustard as a companion crop is recommended; soil treatment with Borax at 15 kg/ha is beneficial; Trichoderma culture (10 kg/ha, CFU 10⁷ per g) is beneficial; roguing of infected clumps should be practiced.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - ಸಮಯ: 2026‑07‑26 16:40 UTC (node demo‑node‑1) - ತಾಪಮಾನ: 23.0 °C (ಬದಲಾವಣೆ ಇಲ್ಲ) - ಆರ್ದ್ರತೆ: 64.2 % (ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಇಳಿದಿದೆ) - ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ತೇವಾಂಶ: 0.0 % (ವಾಲ್ಯೂಮೆಟ್ರಿಕ್) – ಹಿಂದಿನ ಮಾಪನದಿಂದ 49 % ಕುಸಿತ (49 % → 0 %) - ಸಕ್ರಿಯ ಎಚ್ಚರಿಕೆ: **Low ...\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Vision: ಚಿತ್ರವನ್ನು ಬ್ರೌನ್ ಸ್ಪಾಟ್ (brown spot) ಎಂದು 95% ವಿಶ್ವಾಸದೊಂದಿಗೆ ವರ್ಗೀಕರಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ. - Temperature: 25.0 °C - Humidity: 55.0 % - Soil moisture: 40.0 % - Active alerts: - [warning] High humidity 88 % (ರೋಗದ ಅಪಾಯ) - ...\nFarmer: Nodi anna, sugarcane borer training IISR yavaga nadesidru confirm madi.\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** ಪಠ್ಯದಲ್ಲಿ “One‑day Farmer Training Programme on ‘Identification and Management of Major Sugarcane Borers’” ಎಂದು ICAR‑IISR Biological Control Centre, Pravaranagar (Maharashtra) ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಡೆಸಲಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ಉಲ್ಲೇಖಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ, ಆದರೆ ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nRatoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "farmer": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "sarvam": "Cause of chlorosis in rotted crop and suggest actions to overcome it.", "groq": "Causes of chlorosis in sugarcane ratoon crop and management practices to reduce it"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8366, "sarvam": 0.8694, "groq": 0.8984}, "retrieved": [{"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary", "score": 0.8984, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8957, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information", "score": 0.8882, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "diseases/01_disease_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Disease Management — Main Information", "score": 0.8693, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- Agricultural knowledge states that iron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous (high‑pH) soils.  \n- It “worsens in successive ratoon crops.”  \n- Six documented treatments for lime‑induced iron chlorosis are listed, including foliar sprays (e.g., 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea) and soil applications (e.g., FeSO₄ 25 kg ha⁻¹, press‑mud 5 t ha⁻¹ + iron pyrite 2 t ha⁻¹, FYM 25 t ha⁻¹ + foliar FeSO₄ 1.5 % + urea 1 % weekly, elemental S 30–40 kg ha⁻¹, Fe‑EDTA 50 ppm).  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- In ratoon cycles, lime‑induced high soil pH and the buildup of residues reduce iron availability to the plant, leading to more frequent and severe chlorosis.  \n\n**Confidence** – **High** (the knowledge source directly explains the cause and lists proven treatments).  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. Apply a foliar spray of **2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea**, repeating **2–3 times** at 7‑day intervals.  \n2. Apply **FeSO₄ 25 kg ha⁻¹** to the soil (broadcast and incorporate) as a complementary soil‑based correction.  \n3. If FYM is available, incorporate **25 t ha⁻¹** and follow with weekly foliar sprays of **1.5 % FeSO₄ + 1 % urea**; add **1 % ZnSO₄** monthly as per the fourth documented treatment.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Inspect younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing (early chlorosis sign).  \n- If possible, collect a soil sample and test for iron (critical threshold 4.2–6.3 ppm) and pH; re‑test after each application cycle.  \n- Record the number of foliar sprays applied and any visual improvement.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If chlorosis persists after **two** foliar applications and the soil Fe test remains below the critical threshold.  \n- If other nutrient deficiency symptoms appear (e.g., stunted growth, necrosis) or if soil pH is > 7.5 despite amendments.  \n- When planning long‑term soil amendment (lime neutralisation, gypsum, etc.) beyond the listed treatments.", "claims": ["supported: Iron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane", "supported: Iron chlorosis worsens in successive ratoon crops", "supported: Six documented treatments for lime‑induced iron chlorosis exist", "supported: Foliar spray of 2% FeSO4 + 0.5% MnSO4 + 2% urea is a documented treatment", "supported: Foliar spray of 2% FeSO4 + 0.5% MnSO4 + 2% urea should be applied 2–3 times", "supported: Soil application of FeSO4 at 25 kg/ha is a documented treatment", "supported: Soil application of press mud 5 t/ha + iron pyrite 2 t/ha is a documented treatment", "supported: Soil application of farm yard manure 25 t/ha + foliar application of 1.5% FeSO4 + 1% urea weekly, and 1% ZnSO4 monthly is a documented treatment", "supported: Soil application of elemental sulphur 30–40 kg/ha 3 weeks before planting is a documented treatment", "supported: Foliar spray of Fe‑EDTA 50 ppm solution is a documented treatment", "unsupported: Calcareous or high‑pH soils are common in parts of Karnataka's black‑soil tracts", "supported: Chlorosis symptoms include interveinal yellowing of younger leaves", "supported: Apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application on calcareous soils", "supported: Soil testing against critical thresholds (Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm) should guide correction", "supported: During drought, proactively apply FeSO4+ZnSO4+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for symptoms", "supported: Apply foliar spray of 2% FeSO4 + 0.5% MnSO4 + 2% urea, repeating 2–3 times at 7‑day intervals", "supported: Apply FeSO4 25 kg/ha to the soil as complementary correction", "supported: If FYM is available, incorporate 25 t/ha and follow with weekly foliar sprays of 1.5% FeSO4 + 1% urea; add 1% ZnSO4 monthly", "supported: Inspect younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing as early chlorosis sign", "supported: Collect a soil sample and test for iron (critical threshold 4.2–6.3 ppm) and pH; re‑test after each application cycle", "supported: Record the number of foliar sprays applied and any visual improvement"], "unsupported": ["unsupported: Calcareous or high‑pH soils are common in parts of Karnataka's black‑soil tracts"], "final_answer": "Please double-check before acting: \"Calcareous or high‑pH soils are common in parts of Karnataka's black‑soil tracts\" (not in Gage's sources).\n\n**Observation**  \n- Agricultural knowledge states that iron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous (high‑pH) soils.  \n- It “worsens in successive ratoon crops.”  \n- Six documented treatments for lime‑induced iron chlorosis are listed, including foliar sprays (e.g., 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea) and soil applications (e.g., FeSO₄ 25 kg ha⁻¹, press‑mud 5 t ha⁻¹ + iron pyrite 2 t ha⁻¹, FYM 25 t ha⁻¹ + foliar FeSO₄ 1.5 % + urea 1 % weekly, elemental S 30–40 kg ha⁻¹, Fe‑EDTA 50 ppm).  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- In ratoon cycles, lime‑induced high soil pH and the buildup of residues reduce iron availability to the plant, leading to more frequent and severe chlorosis.  \n\n**Confidence** – **High** (the knowledge source directly explains the cause and lists proven treatments).  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. Apply a foliar spray of **2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea**, repeating **2–3 times** at 7‑day intervals.  \n2. Apply **FeSO₄ 25 kg ha⁻¹** to the soil (broadcast and incorporate) as a complementary soil‑based correction.  \n3. If FYM is available, incorporate **25 t ha⁻¹** and follow with weekly foliar sprays of **1.5 % FeSO₄ + 1 % urea**; add **1 % ZnSO₄** monthly as per the fourth documented treatment.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Inspect younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing (early chlorosis sign).  \n- If possible, collect a soil sample and test for iron (critical threshold 4.2–6.3 ppm) and pH; re‑test after each application cycle.  \n- Record the number of foliar sprays applied and any visual improvement.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If chlorosis persists after **two** foliar applications and the soil Fe test remains below the critical threshold.  \n- If other nutrient deficiency symptoms appear (e.g., stunted growth, necrosis) or if soil pH is > 7.5 despite amendments.  \n- When planning long‑term soil amendment (lime neutralisation, gypsum, etc.) beyond the listed treatments.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 1.46, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 207, "completion_tokens": 92, "seconds": 0.63, "status": "ok", "seq": 4}, {"purpose": "answer", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "prompt_tokens": 2008, "completion_tokens": 1141, "seconds": 2.77, "status": "ok", "seq": 5}, {"purpose": "fact_check", "model"