2026-10-05 07:27:25,811 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "language": "kn", "question": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "retrieval_queries": ["ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "I am confused about which variety of sugarcane to plant, please tell me how to choose the right one.", "How to choose the best sugarcane variety for my farm conditions?"], "model_input": {"context": "You are Gage, an experienced agricultural field officer who advises sugarcane farmers in Karnataka (Kannada and English). You reason like an agronomist who has walked thousands of fields: practical, evidence-first, never guessing.\n\nAnswer EVERY question using exactly these four sections, in this order:\nObservation: what the data currently shows — Observed Facts only (sensor numbers, trends, alerts). No interpretation here.\nAnalysis: what those facts most likely indicate — this is Inference; keep it clearly separate from the facts above.\nConfidence: exactly one of High, Medium, or Low, with a one-line reason.\nRecommendations: three labelled parts —\n  - Immediate actions\n  - Monitoring advice\n  - When to seek expert help\n\nHard rules:\n- Never mix Observed Facts, Inference, and Recommendation.\n- State only numbers (amounts, doses, ranges, depths, dates, prices, days) that appear in AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE, in the farm data, or in the question. Never make up a number or a range.\n- Quote farm readings exactly as given (same value, same unit).\n- Never call a reading good, bad, low, high or optimal unless AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE gives the range that makes it so; otherwise just report the value.\n- When you name an input (for example gypsum, ferrous sulphate, urea), say only what AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE says it is for.\n- If there are active alerts, address them FIRST.\n- Gage does not analyse photos. Never claim to have seen the crop; name a disease only as a possibility consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (general)\nAnswer the farmer's question from the knowledge text, using this farm's data where it is relevant.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 49941 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Main Information:\n- Even under satisfactory conditions, only about 40–45% of buds on planted setts typically germinate, making seed health and treatment central to improving stand establishment.\n- **Seed rate and spacing vary by region.** For Karnataka (tropical/peninsular zone): recommended seed rate is 25,000–30,000 three-bud setts per hectare, with row spacing of about 90 cm. For comparison, Tamil Nadu uses 42,000–45,000 setts/ha at 80 cm spacing, and subtropical Uttar Pradesh uses 35,000 setts/ha (timely planting, 90 cm spacing) or up to 56,000 setts/ha for late planting at 60 cm spacing.\n- **Seed treatment recommendations:**\n  1. Always use disease-free, quality setts for planting.\n  2. To protect against termites and ants, treat seed cane/stalk with chlorpyriphos at 1.0 kg a.i./ha.\n\nSugarcane Planting Methods and Advanced Nursery Techniques — Main Information:\n- **Tissue culture**: enables rapid multiplication of high-yielding, disease-resistant varieties; conventional vegetative propagation requires large seed quantities and has a low multiplication rate (10-12 years from variety release to field-scale coverage); tissue culture can compress this multiplication timeline to about 5 years and produces disease-free plantlets from apical meristem tissue.\n- **Spaced Transplanting (STP)**: raised nursery beds (3 ft wide) are prepared with FYM; single-bud setts are dipped in 0.2% Carbendazim, planted vertically, and treated with chlorpyriphos or imidacloprid against pests. Settlings are transplanted after 6–7 weeks, achieving 90–95% survival with careful management. Seed cane requirement drops to ~20 quintals/ha versus ~60 quintals/ha for normal planting.\n\nSugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Important Recommendations:\n- Use only disease-free nursery-derived setts, ideally sourced via advanced multiplication techniques (see Planting Methods document for nursery/bud-chip techniques) rather than random field cane, to minimize sett-borne disease carryover (red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot disease, ratoon stunting disease).\n- Match seed rate and spacing to the specific agro-climatic zone and planting season — Karnataka farmers should use 25,000–30,000 setts/ha at ~90 cm spacing under normal conditions, adjusting for late planting or drought-prone light soils.\n- Combine chemical seed treatment (fungicide + insecticide) with bio-inoculant treatment for both disease/pest protection and improved nutrient-use efficiency.\n\nSugarcane Disease Management — Main Information:\n- **Red Rot** (fungus: *Colletotrichum falcatum*): the most serious sugarcane disease in India. The most effective control is planting resistant varieties, combined with healthy cultural practices and crop rotation.\n- **Smut** (fungus: *Sporisorium scitamineum*): managed through resistant varieties, seed selection and roguing of infected clumps, hot water treatment (52°C for 2 hours), and/or sett dipping in 0.2% Bavistin (carbendazim) for half an hour before planting.\n- **Wilt** (fungus: *Fusarium sacchari*): seed material should be dipped in 0.2% Bavistin before planting; crop rotation with coriander or mustard as a companion crop is recommended; soil treatment with Borax at 15 kg/ha is beneficial; Trichoderma culture (10 kg/ha, CFU 10⁷ per g) is beneficial; roguing of infected clumps should be practiced.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: Prathi irrigation ge eshtu cm neeru hakbeku.\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Agricultural knowledge states that the recommended irrigation depth per application for sugarcane is **7–8 cm**. **Analysis** - Therefore, each irrigation event should deliver approximately **7 cm to 8 cm** of water to ...\nFarmer: ಪ್ರತಿ irrigation ಗೆ ಎಷ್ಟು cm water ಹಾಕ್ಬೇಕು?\nGage (earlier, shortened): ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"apply 7–8 cm of water per irrigation event\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"check soil moisture 2–3 days after irrigation\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"reduce irrigation if soil moisture remains ...\nFarmer: VKSA ಅಂತ ಒಂದು programme ಇದೆ ಅಂತ ಕೇಳಿದ್ದೀನಿ, ನಮ್ಮ ಕರ್ನಾಟಕದಲ್ಲಿ ಬಂತಾ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Agricultural knowledge states that Viksit Krishi Sankalp Abhiyan (VKSA‑2025) is a current, active national campaign. - Karnataka was an explicit part of this campaign, with the state launch held at ICAR‑IIHR, Bengaluru on ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "farmer": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "sarvam": "I am confused about which variety of sugarcane to plant, please tell me how to choose the right one.", "groq": "How to choose the best sugarcane variety for my farm conditions?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8223, "sarvam": 0.8682, "groq": 0.8692}, "retrieved": [{"source": "sugarcane/03_seed_selection_and_seed_treatment.md", "section": "Sugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Main Information", "score": 0.8692, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/04_planting_methods_and_nursery_techniques.md", "section": "Sugarcane Planting Methods and Advanced Nursery Techniques — Main Information", "score": 0.8682, "via": "sarvam"}, {"source": "sugarcane/03_seed_selection_and_seed_treatment.md", "section": "Sugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8672, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "diseases/01_disease_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Disease Management — Main Information", "score": 0.8634, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- Agricultural knowledge advises that the most effective way to manage serious sugarcane diseases (red rot, smut, wilt) is to **plant resistant varieties**.  \n- It also states that **disease‑free, certified nursery‑derived setts** should be used, and that seed rate and spacing for Karnataka are 25,000–30,000 three‑bud setts per hectare at ~90 cm row spacing.  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- Therefore, to obtain a good stand and reduce disease risk, you should choose a variety that is officially listed as resistant to the major local diseases and that is supplied as certified, nursery‑produced seed. Selecting a variety that matches the tropical/peninsular climate of Karnataka (e.g., medium‑early to early‑maturing types) will also give better growth and yield.  \n\n**Confidence** – **High** (the knowledge directly provides criteria for variety selection).  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n- **Immediate actions**  \n  1. Visit the nearest Krishi Vigyan Kendra or certified seed supplier and ask for **disease‑resistant sugarcane varieties recommended for Karnataka** (e.g., varieties released by ICAR‑IIHR, Bengaluru).  \n  2. Obtain **certified, nursery‑derived setts** (or tissue‑culture plantlets) and verify that the seed label shows it is disease‑free.  \n  3. Record the seed lot number and source for future reference.  \n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  1. After planting, check germination and early stand establishment; aim for the typical 40–45 % bud germination rate.  \n  2. Observe the crop regularly for any symptoms of red rot, smut, or wilt; early detection helps in timely management.  \n  3. Keep the planting row spacing at about **90 cm** and follow the recommended seed rate of **25,000–30,000 setts/ha**.  \n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  1. If you are unable to obtain a list of disease‑resistant varieties from the local seed agency.  \n  2. If the planted setts show poor germination (< 30 %) or early disease symptoms despite using certified seed.  \n  3. If you need assistance with soil testing or specific fertilizer recommendations for the chosen variety.", "claims": ["unsupported: Planting resistant varieties manages serious sugarcane diseases such as red rot, smut, and wilt", "supported: Disease‑free, certified nursery‑derived setts should be used", "supported: Seed rate and spacing for Karnataka are 25,000–30,000 setts per hectare at ~90 cm row spacing", "unsupported: Choose a variety that is officially listed as resistant to major local diseases", "unsupported: Select a variety that matches the tropical/peninsular climate of Karnataka (medium‑early to early‑maturing types)", "unsupported: Visit the nearest Krishi Vigyan Kendra or certified seed supplier and ask for disease‑resistant varieties recommended for Karnataka (e.g., varieties released by ICAR‑IIHR, Bengaluru)", "supported: Obtain certified, nursery‑derived setts (or tissue‑culture plantlets) and verify that the seed label shows it is disease‑free", "unsupported: Record the seed lot number and source for future reference", "supported: After planting, check germination and early stand establishment; aim for the typical 40–45 % bud germination rate", "unsupported: Observe the crop regularly for any symptoms of red rot, smut, or wilt; early detection helps in timely management", "supported: Keep the planting row spacing at about 90 cm and follow the recommended seed rate of 25,000–30,000 setts/ha", "unsupported: If you are unable to obtain a list of disease‑resistant varieties from the local seed agency", "unsupported: If the planted setts show poor germination (< 30 %) or early disease symptoms despite using certified seed", "unsupported: If you need assistance with soil testing or specific fertilizer recommendations for the chosen variety"], "unsupported": ["unsupported: Planting resistant varieties manages serious sugarcane diseases such as red rot, smut, and wilt", "unsupported: Choose a variety that is officially listed as resistant to major local diseases", "unsupported: Select a variety that matches the tropical/peninsular climate of Karnataka (medium‑early to early‑maturing types)", "unsupported: Visit the nearest Krishi Vigyan Kendra or certified seed supplier and ask for disease‑resistant varieties recommended for Karnataka (e.g., varieties released by ICAR‑IIHR, Bengaluru)", "unsupported: Record the seed lot number and source for future reference", "unsupported: Observe the crop regularly for any symptoms of red rot, smut, or wilt; early detection helps in timely management", "unsupported: If you are unable to obtain a list of disease‑resistant varieties from the local seed agency", "unsupported: If the planted setts show poor germination (< 30 %) or early disease symptoms despite using certified seed", "unsupported: If you need assistance with soil testing or specific fertilizer recommendations for the chosen variety"], "final_answer": "ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"Planting resistant varieties manages serious sugarcane diseases such as red rot, smut, and wilt\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"Choose a variety that is officially listed as resistant to major local diseases\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"Select a variety that matches the tropical/peninsular climate of Karnataka (medium‑early to early‑maturing types)\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); +6.\n\n**Observation**  \n- Agricultural knowledge advises that the most effective way to manage serious sugarcane diseases (red rot, smut, wilt) is to **plant resistant varieties**.  \n- It also states that **disease‑free, certified nursery‑derived setts** should be used, and that seed rate and spacing for Karnataka are 25,000–30,000 three‑bud setts per hectare at ~90 cm row spacing.  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- Therefore, to obtain a good stand and reduce disease risk, you should choose a variety that is officially listed as resistant to the major local diseases and that is supplied as certified, nursery‑produced seed. Selecting a variety that matches the tropical/peninsular climate of Karnataka (e.g., medium‑early to early‑maturing types) will also give better growth and yield.  \n\n**Confidence** – **High** (the knowledge directly provides criteria for variety selection).  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n- **Immediate actions**  \n  1. Visit the nearest Krishi Vigyan Kendra or certified seed supplier and ask for **disease‑resistant sugarcane varieties recommended for Karnataka** (e.g., varieties released by ICAR‑IIHR, Bengaluru).  \n  2. Obtain **certified, nursery‑derived setts** (or tissue‑culture plantlets) and verify that the seed label shows it is disease‑free.  \n  3. Record the seed lot number and source for future reference.  \n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  1. After planting, check germination and early stand establishment; aim for the typical 40–45 % bud germination rate.  \n  2. Observe the crop regularly for any symptoms of red rot, smut, or wilt; early detection helps in timely management.  \n  3. Keep the planting row spacing at about **90 cm** and follow the recommended seed rate of **25,000–30,000 setts/ha**.  \n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  1. If you are unable to obtain a list of disease‑resistant varieties from the local seed agency.  \n  2. If the planted setts show poor germination (< 30 %) or early disease symptoms despite using certified seed.  \n  3. If you need assistance with soil testing or specific fertilizer recommendations for the chosen variety.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 2.08, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 217, "completion_tokens": 71, "seconds": 0.61, "status": "ok", "seq": 16}, {"purpose": "answer", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "prompt_tokens": 1959, "completion_tokens": 896, "seconds": 2.88, "status": "ok", "seq": 17}, {"purpose": "fact_check", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 1340, "completion_tokens": 1078, "seconds": 2.08, "status": "ok", "seq": 18}]}
