ernal weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (general)\nAnswer the farmer's question from the knowledge text, using this farm's data where it is relevant.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 49941 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Market Price: Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) System — Important Recommendations:\n- Sugarcane farmers should track the **announced FRP for the current sugar season** (October–September cycle) rather than relying on the previous year's figure, since FRP is revised annually and the applicable rate is announced before each 1 October start of the crushing season.\n- Because payment is legally due within 14 days of delivery, farmers experiencing payment delays beyond this window have a formal basis to escalate to the sugar mill/state cane commissioner, since delayed payment triggers a statutory interest liability on the mill.\n- Farmers should check whether their state has fixed a State Advised Price (SAP) higher than the central FRP, since where a SAP exists and exceeds FRP, mills are obligated to pay the higher SAP rate.\n\nSugarcane Market Price: Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) System — Main Information:\n- **What FRP is**: the minimum price that sugar mills must legally pay to sugarcane farmers (\"Gannakisan\") for cane supplied for crushing, for a given basic sugar recovery rate. It replaced the earlier Statutory Minimum Price (SMP) concept following an amendment to the Sugarcane (Control) Order, 1966, on 22 October 2009.\n- **Legal basis**: FRP operates under the Essential Commodities Act, 1955, and the Sugarcane (Control) Order, 1966, ensuring price parity and farmer protection irrespective of whether individual sugar mills are profitable.\n- **How it is set**: recommended by the CACP (an attached office of the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare, established January 1965), in consultation with state governments and sugar industry associations, and finally approved by the CCEA.\n\nSugarcane Market Price: Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) System — Summary:\nUnlike most agricultural commodities that rely purely on open-market price discovery, sugarcane in India is protected by a statutory minimum price mechanism — the Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) — which sugar mills are legally required to pay farmers. The FRP is announced annually by the Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) before each crushing season, based on Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP) recommendations.\n\nSugarcane Market Price: Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) System — Main Information:\n- **Recovery-linked premium/penalty structure**: FRP is fixed for a \"basic recovery rate\" (currently 10.25%), with a premium added for every 0.1% of recovery above this rate, and a corresponding reduction for every 0.1% below it. However, the government has built in a floor: **no deduction is applied where a mill's recovery falls below 9.5%** — farmers supplying such mills still receive a guaranteed minimum FRP.\n- **Historical FRP trend (₹/quintal, at 10.25% recovery)**: ₹210 (2013-14) → ₹220 (2014-15) → ₹230 (2015-16, 2016-17) → ₹255 (2017-18) → ₹275 (2018-19, 2019-20) → ₹285 (2020-21) → ₹290 (2021-22) → ₹305 (2022-23) → ₹315 (2023-24) → ₹340 (2024-25) → ₹355 (2025-26) → ₹365 (2026-27, approved).\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: VKSA ಅಂತ ಒಂದು programme ಇದೆ ಅಂತ ಕೇಳಿದ್ದೀನಿ, ನಮ್ಮ ಕರ್ನಾಟಕದಲ್ಲಿ ಬಂತಾ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Agricultural knowledge states that Viksit Krishi Sankalp Abhiyan (VKSA‑2025) is a current, active national campaign. - Karnataka was an explicit part of this campaign, with the state launch held at ICAR‑IIHR, Bengaluru on ...\nFarmer: ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.\nGage (earlier, shortened): ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"Planting resistant varieties manages serious sugarcane diseases such as red rot, smut, and wilt\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"Choose a variety that is officially listed as resistant to major local ...\nFarmer: Sir every time watering how much depth I give, any standard number?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"regardless of specific field conditions\" (not in Gage's sources); \"observe soil‑moisture sensor a few days later to confirm moisture increased\" (not in Gage's sources); \"adjust next irrigation if soil ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nWhat is the FRP for sugarcane this season and how soon must the mill pay?", "farmer": "What is the FRP for sugarcane this season and how soon must the mill pay?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "What is the FRP for sugarcane this season and how soon must the mill pay?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.9058}, "retrieved": [{"source": "market/01_sugarcane_fair_and_remunerative_price_frp.md", "section": "Sugarcane Market Price: Fair and Remunera