nditions, standard advisory content observed includes:\n  - Farmers advised to irrigate rice, banana, **sugarcane**, pomegranate, and other water-dependent crops.\n  - Mulching between crop plants with crop debris is advised to maintain optimum soil moisture.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations:\n- Farmers on calcareous or high-pH soils (common in parts of Karnataka's black-soil tracts) should watch for chlorosis symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information:\n- Critical soil deficiency thresholds identified in the source: Fe (non-calcareous soil) below 4.2 ppm; Fe (calcareous soil) below 6.3 ppm; Zn (loamy soils) below 1.2 ppm; Zn (clay soils) below 2.0 ppm; Mn below 2.0 ppm; Cu below 1.2 ppm; hot-water-soluble Boron below 0.44 ppm.\n- Beyond iron, cane growth and development are also affected by boron, copper, sulphur, magnesium, manganese, molybdenum, and zinc deficiencies; while needed in small quantities, these are essential where soil deficiencies exist. Calcareous soils in particular require attention to zinc and sulphur status.\n- Where multiple micronutrient deficiencies exist, a combined basal application of 25 kg Ferrous Sulphate, 20 kg Zinc Sulphate, 10 kg Manganese Sulphate, 10 kg Copper Sulphate, 2.5 kg Sodium Molybdate, and 5 kg Borax per hectare, mixed with well-decomposed manure or compost, is advocated.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information:\n- Iron chlorosis has been observed in nearly all sugarcane-growing states of India, particularly Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, and Bihar; it is most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.\n- **Six documented treatments for lime-induced iron chlorosis:**\n  1. Foliar spray of 2% FeSO₄ solution combined with 0.5% MnSO₄ and 2% urea, applied two to three times.\n  2. Soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg/ha.\n  3. Soil application of press mud (5 t/ha) combined with iron pyrite (2 t/ha).\n  4. Soil application of farm yard manure (25 t/ha) plus foliar application of 1.5% FeSO₄ with 1% urea at weekly intervals, and 1% ZnSO₄ at monthly intervals.\n  5. Soil application of elemental sulphur at 30–40 kg/ha, applied 3 weeks before planting.\n  6. Foliar spray of Fe-EDTA (50 ppm solution).\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: Sir every time watering how much depth I give, any standard number?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"regardless of specific field conditions\" (not in Gage's sources); \"observe soil‑moisture sensor a few days later to confirm moisture increased\" (not in Gage's sources); \"adjust next irrigation if soil ...\nFarmer: What is the FRP for sugarcane this season and how soon must the mill pay?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - The knowledge text states the approved FRP for the 2026‑27 sugarcane season is **₹365 per quintal**. - It also states that **payment is legally due within 14 days of delivery** of the cane. **Analysis** - The current ...\nFarmer: Why are the leaves of my cane turning yellow?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"The current soil‑moisture reading of 32.3% is not enough to confirm adequate moisture\" (not in Gage's sources); \"Water the field to raise soil moisture to a level that keeps the root zone moist (aim for ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nIs my soil too dry right now?", "farmer": "Is my soil too dry right now?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Is my soil too dry right now?", "sarvam": "Is my soil too dry?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8368, "sarvam": 0.8296}, "retrieved": [{"source": "weather/01_agromet_advisory_north_karnataka.md", "section": "Weather and Agromet Advisory for North Karnataka (IMD, Sample Bulletin) — Main Information", "score": 0.8368, "via": "original"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8334, "via": "original"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information", "score": 0.8315, "via": "original"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information", "score": 0.831, "via": "original"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- Active alert: **[critical] Node offline (no contact for 49941 min)**.  \n- Latest sensor reading (2026‑09‑23 04:25 UTC): **Soil moisture: 32.3 %**.  \n- No other recent soil‑moisture data for trend comparison.\n\n**Analysis**  \n- The node‑offline alert means the system has not received any data for over 34 days, so the 32.3 % reading is the only moisture information available.  \n- Agricultural knowledge does not provide a specific optimal soil‑moisture percentage for sugarcane at the current growth stage, so we cannot definitively label 32.3 % as “too dry” or “adequate.”  \n- Without a known reference range or recent trend, the dryness status remains uncertain.\n\n**Confidence**  \n- **Low** – because the required benchmark for sugarcane soil‑moisture is not available and the sensor has been offline for an extended period.\n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. **Restore node connectivity** – check power, antenna, and network link to bring the sensor back online.  \n2. **Visually inspect the field** – feel the soil at root depth (≈30 cm). If the soil feels dry or cracks, consider applying irrigation to maintain a moist root zone.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Once the node is online, record soil‑moisture readings at least every 24 h to establish a baseline and trend.  \n- Compare future readings with field observations (soil feel, plant vigor) to develop a practical moisture range for your specific field.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If the node cannot be re‑connected after basic troubleshooting.  \n- If visual inspection shows persistent dryness despite irrigation, or if cane leaves begin showing stress symptoms (e.g., yellowing) and you need a detailed moisture‑deficit assessment.", "claims": ["unsupported: Node offline alert means no data for over 34 days", "unsupported: 32.3% reading is the only moisture information available", "unsupported: No known optimal soil‑moisture percentage for sugarcane at current growth stage", "unsupported: Cannot label 32.3% as too dry or adequate", "unsupported: Dryness status remains uncertain", "unsupported: Restore node connectivity – check power, antenna, network link", "unsupported: Visually inspect field – feel soil at root depth (~30 cm)", "unsupported: If soil feels dry or cracks, consider applying irrigation to maintain moist root zone", "unsupported: Record soil‑moisture readings at least every 24 h to establish baseline", "unsupported: Compare future readings with field observations to develop practical moisture range", "unsupported: Seek expert hel